1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,590
You have around thirty trillion cells.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:05,670
Every single one of them contains DNA.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,030
And every time one of those cells divides,

4
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:11,330
there is a chance that
something goes wrong.

5
00:00:12,084 --> 00:00:15,514
A mutation. Most mutations are
harmless. Some are repaired.

6
00:00:16,024 --> 00:00:17,294
Some are simply irrelevant.

7
00:00:18,198 --> 00:00:22,338
But occasionally, a mutation gives a cell
the ability to grow when it shouldn't.

8
00:00:22,418 --> 00:00:26,468
To ignore the body's
instructions. To keep dividing.

9
00:00:26,658 --> 00:00:29,088
And eventually, to become cancer.

10
00:00:30,053 --> 00:00:31,843
Now here's the strange part.

11
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:35,013
You might expect that the
biggest animals, with the most

12
00:00:35,093 --> 00:00:40,083
cells and the longest lifespans, would
have the most cancer. But they don't.

13
00:00:40,711 --> 00:00:44,821
And one of the most fascinating examples
is standing right in front of us.

14
00:00:45,151 --> 00:00:46,351
An elephant.

15
00:00:46,887 --> 00:00:50,997
An African elephant can weigh several
tonnes and can live for many decades.

16
00:00:51,787 --> 00:00:55,097
Compared with a human, it has
an enormous number of cells.

17
00:00:56,009 --> 00:01:00,119
And every one of those cells is another
potential starting point for cancer.

18
00:01:01,033 --> 00:01:04,013
So if every cell had roughly
the same probability of becoming

19
00:01:04,093 --> 00:01:07,603
cancerous, elephants should
be walking cancer factories.

20
00:01:08,638 --> 00:01:12,138
But observations across mammals
don't show the simple relationship

21
00:01:12,218 --> 00:01:17,308
we might expect between body size,
lifespan, and cancer mortality.

22
00:01:18,185 --> 00:01:20,295
This is known as Peto's paradox.

23
00:01:20,485 --> 00:01:23,195
And it raises a remarkable question.

24
00:01:24,043 --> 00:01:27,243
How did evolution allow huge
animals to live for so long without

25
00:01:27,323 --> 00:01:32,073
cancer becoming an inevitable
consequence of having so many cells?

26
00:01:32,971 --> 00:01:35,961
Elephants appear to have evolved
several layers of protection.

27
00:01:36,511 --> 00:01:39,661
And one of the most famous
involves something called TP53.

28
00:01:43,491 --> 00:01:48,901
But before we talk about TP53, we need
to understand what a gene actually is.

29
00:01:49,667 --> 00:01:51,967
When people hear the word gene, they often

30
00:01:52,047 --> 00:01:55,357
imagine a tiny piece of
DNA with a label on it.

31
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:58,155
But a gene isn't really
a little object sitting

32
00:01:58,235 --> 00:02:01,425
inside the cell with a
job description attached.

33
00:02:02,424 --> 00:02:05,164
DNA is a huge molecular
information system.

34
00:02:05,244 --> 00:02:09,684
And a gene is a particular
region of that DNA, containing

35
00:02:09,764 --> 00:02:13,554
information the cell can use to
produce a functional product.

36
00:02:14,383 --> 00:02:15,783
Often, that product is a protein.

37
00:02:15,863 --> 00:02:20,603
And proteins are the machines of the
cell. They build things. Transport things.

38
00:02:20,683 --> 00:02:21,883
Send signals.

39
00:02:22,543 --> 00:02:24,713
So here's a useful simplification.

40
00:02:25,583 --> 00:02:27,113
DNA is the information.

41
00:02:28,283 --> 00:02:31,553
A gene is a specific instruction
encoded in that information.

42
00:02:32,542 --> 00:02:36,392
A protein is often the molecular
machine produced from that instruction.

43
00:02:37,542 --> 00:02:42,632
And this distinction becomes extremely
important when we talk about TP53.

44
00:02:43,477 --> 00:02:45,277
Because TP53 is the gene.

45
00:02:45,357 --> 00:02:48,807
And p53 is the protein produced from it.

46
00:02:49,517 --> 00:02:51,027
They are not the same thing.

47
00:02:53,317 --> 00:02:56,627
Inside your cells is a protein called p53.

48
00:02:57,677 --> 00:03:01,207
It is one of the most important
tumour-suppressing proteins we know.

49
00:03:01,927 --> 00:03:06,477
You can think of it as one of the cell's
security systems. Imagine a factory.

50
00:03:07,506 --> 00:03:10,196
If one of the machines
starts behaving dangerously,

51
00:03:10,566 --> 00:03:12,716
someone has to stop the production line.

52
00:03:13,386 --> 00:03:16,256
That's roughly what p53 can help do.

53
00:03:16,991 --> 00:03:19,731
When a cell experiences
serious stress, including

54
00:03:19,811 --> 00:03:24,201
DNA damage, the p53 pathway
can become activated.

55
00:03:25,172 --> 00:03:28,922
p53 can then switch on genes
that stop the cell cycle.

56
00:03:29,532 --> 00:03:32,402
Genes that give the cell
time to repair its DNA.

57
00:03:32,892 --> 00:03:37,242
Or, if the damage is too severe,
genes that trigger apoptosis.

58
00:03:38,115 --> 00:03:41,225
Apoptosis is essentially
programmed cell death.

59
00:03:42,095 --> 00:03:44,385
The cell deliberately dismantles itself.

60
00:03:45,253 --> 00:03:48,233
Not because the organism has
failed. Quite the opposite.

61
00:03:48,313 --> 00:03:49,763
It's a sacrifice.

62
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:55,540
One damaged cell dies so that the organism
doesn't have to deal with a tumour later.

63
00:03:58,410 --> 00:04:02,220
Now imagine what happens when this
security system stops working.

64
00:04:03,110 --> 00:04:04,720
A cell acquires mutations.

65
00:04:05,697 --> 00:04:08,517
Normally, damage-control systems
should detect the problem.

66
00:04:08,597 --> 00:04:13,907
But suppose the cell also loses an
important tumour-suppressor mechanism.

67
00:04:14,798 --> 00:04:16,988
Now the cell can continue dividing.

68
00:04:17,958 --> 00:04:19,968
More divisions create more mutations.

69
00:04:20,998 --> 00:04:24,608
Some of those mutations make the
cell even better at surviving.

70
00:04:25,309 --> 00:04:27,349
Eventually, you can get
a population of cells

71
00:04:27,429 --> 00:04:31,439
that no longer behaves like
normal tissue. That's cancer.

72
00:04:32,402 --> 00:04:36,142
And TP53 is so important
that mutations affecting

73
00:04:36,222 --> 00:04:39,092
it occur in a huge
fraction of human cancers.

74
00:04:40,093 --> 00:04:42,473
The National Cancer Institute
estimates that roughly

75
00:04:42,553 --> 00:04:47,743
half of human cancers have
TP53 mutations or deletions.

76
00:04:48,573 --> 00:04:53,003
So p53 is one of the central
guardians against cancer in our cells.

77
00:04:55,793 --> 00:04:59,143
So how many TP53 genes do humans have?

78
00:04:59,893 --> 00:05:01,863
Here's where the story gets interesting.

79
00:05:02,781 --> 00:05:06,211
A typical human cell
has two copies of TP53.

80
00:05:07,041 --> 00:05:10,691
One inherited from your
mother. One from your father.

81
00:05:11,457 --> 00:05:13,817
So when people say that
elephants have twenty copies

82
00:05:13,897 --> 00:05:18,147
of TP53 while humans have
one, that's misleading.

83
00:05:19,084 --> 00:05:24,714
Humans have one TP53 gene location, but
normally two copies, or two alleles.

84
00:05:27,204 --> 00:05:33,214
African elephants, on the other hand, have
a remarkable expansion of the TP53 family.

85
00:05:33,956 --> 00:05:36,771
The original studies
identified about twenty

86
00:05:36,851 --> 00:05:40,506
TP53-related copies in
the elephant genome.

87
00:05:41,327 --> 00:05:46,347
That includes the normal TP53
gene plus numerous retrogenes.

88
00:05:47,167 --> 00:05:50,937
Additional copies derived from
an RNA-based copying process.

89
00:05:51,771 --> 00:05:57,301
And some of these elephant TP53-related
sequences are transcriptionally active.

90
00:05:58,161 --> 00:06:03,151
More importantly, elephant cells show
a striking response to DNA damage.

91
00:06:03,851 --> 00:06:06,201
They are much more likely
than normal human cells

92
00:06:06,281 --> 00:06:10,071
to undergo apoptosis
after serious DNA damage.

93
00:06:10,867 --> 00:06:14,807
In one experimental study, elephant
lymphocytes showed substantially

94
00:06:14,887 --> 00:06:19,877
higher rates of apoptosis following
DNA damage than human lymphocytes.

95
00:06:20,819 --> 00:06:23,249
But there's a catch. A
very important catch.

96
00:06:26,359 --> 00:06:31,169
For years, the popular explanation was
essentially this. Elephants are huge.

97
00:06:31,939 --> 00:06:33,649
Huge animals have more cells.

98
00:06:34,359 --> 00:06:36,129
More cells should mean more cancer.

99
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:42,290
So evolution gave elephants twenty
copies of TP53. Problem solved.

100
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:47,030
It's a great story. But biology
is rarely that convenient.

101
00:06:48,063 --> 00:06:50,963
Some of the extra elephant TP53 copies are

102
00:06:51,043 --> 00:06:54,253
retrogenes, and many
of them are truncated.

103
00:06:54,823 --> 00:07:00,153
They are not simply twenty identical
copies of the normal human TP53 gene.

104
00:07:00,918 --> 00:07:05,428
A twenty twenty-two evolutionary
analysis argued something different.

105
00:07:06,358 --> 00:07:08,258
Many of these retrogenes
may have accumulated

106
00:07:08,338 --> 00:07:11,708
largely through duplication
and genetic drift.

107
00:07:12,557 --> 00:07:15,317
Not because natural selection
specifically favoured

108
00:07:15,397 --> 00:07:18,187
every one of them as
an anti-cancer defence.

109
00:07:19,117 --> 00:07:24,307
That doesn't mean the elephant TP53
system is irrelevant. Quite the opposite.

110
00:07:25,315 --> 00:07:28,895
Other experiments indicate
that some elephant TP53-related

111
00:07:29,495 --> 00:07:33,065
sequences can influence
the DNA-damage response.

112
00:07:34,029 --> 00:07:36,609
But it means we shouldn't say
that scientists proved that

113
00:07:36,689 --> 00:07:42,719
twenty copies of TP53 are the reason
elephants don't get cancer. They haven't.

114
00:07:43,341 --> 00:07:45,331
The real story is more interesting.

115
00:07:46,141 --> 00:07:49,091
We are still figuring out
exactly what evolution did.

116
00:07:51,711 --> 00:07:53,461
And then there's the zombie gene.

117
00:07:54,311 --> 00:07:56,621
Elephants have another fascinating trick.

118
00:07:56,711 --> 00:07:58,341
It's called LIF6.

119
00:07:59,169 --> 00:08:03,309
At some point in the evolutionary
history of elephants, a copy of an

120
00:08:03,389 --> 00:08:06,999
old gene that had become inactive
appears to have been repurposed.

121
00:08:07,756 --> 00:08:13,306
When p53 detects serious cellular
damage, LIF6 can be activated.

122
00:08:14,436 --> 00:08:16,476
The resulting protein can
contribute to mitochondrial

123
00:08:16,556 --> 00:08:19,926
damage and push the cell toward apoptosis.

124
00:08:20,761 --> 00:08:24,991
In other words, p53 says one
thing. This cell is damaged.

125
00:08:25,321 --> 00:08:28,991
And LIF6 turns that warning
into something stronger.

126
00:08:29,241 --> 00:08:31,151
Then kill the cell.

127
00:08:31,892 --> 00:08:36,242
It's a beautiful example of evolution
reusing old genetic material.

128
00:08:37,252 --> 00:08:38,992
A gene that had effectively become dormant

129
00:08:39,072 --> 00:08:42,182
was recruited into a
new biological system.

130
00:08:44,892 --> 00:08:48,042
But elephants don't have
one magic anti-cancer gene.

131
00:08:48,912 --> 00:08:51,482
And this is probably the
most important lesson.

132
00:08:52,243 --> 00:08:54,123
Elephants aren't cancer-proof.

133
00:08:54,543 --> 00:08:56,313
They can and do develop cancer.

134
00:08:57,312 --> 00:09:01,172
The famous estimate from one large
study was that cancer accounted

135
00:09:01,252 --> 00:09:05,042
for around four point eight per
cent of recorded elephant mortality.

136
00:09:05,984 --> 00:09:08,144
That is far below the
fraction recorded for

137
00:09:08,224 --> 00:09:10,974
humans in the dataset used by that study.

138
00:09:11,804 --> 00:09:13,834
But we should be careful
with those numbers.

139
00:09:14,637 --> 00:09:19,307
Comparing cancer rates between wild
animals and humans is extremely difficult.

140
00:09:19,977 --> 00:09:22,347
Animals aren't monitored the same way.

141
00:09:22,877 --> 00:09:25,147
Their deaths aren't always investigated.

142
00:09:26,107 --> 00:09:28,117
Some tumours are never detected.

143
00:09:28,507 --> 00:09:33,277
And veterinary records are nowhere near
as complete as human medical records.

144
00:09:34,184 --> 00:09:37,694
So the claim that elephants
never get cancer is simply wrong.

145
00:09:38,504 --> 00:09:42,764
The scientifically interesting statement
is that elephants appear to experience

146
00:09:42,844 --> 00:09:47,754
less cancer than we would expect from
their enormous size and long lifespan.

147
00:09:48,605 --> 00:09:50,735
And that is the real Peto's paradox.

148
00:09:53,735 --> 00:09:56,165
So why don't humans
simply copy the elephant?

149
00:09:57,075 --> 00:09:59,325
This is where things get really exciting.

150
00:10:00,255 --> 00:10:04,365
Imagine we could somehow make human
cells behave more like elephant cells.

151
00:10:04,995 --> 00:10:07,025
More sensitive to DNA damage.

152
00:10:07,735 --> 00:10:09,365
More willing to stop dividing.

153
00:10:10,273 --> 00:10:13,683
More willing to destroy themselves
when something goes badly wrong.

154
00:10:14,513 --> 00:10:17,603
Wouldn't that make us
resistant to cancer? Maybe.

155
00:10:18,541 --> 00:10:21,911
But biology immediately
throws another problem at us.

156
00:10:22,701 --> 00:10:25,671
Your body doesn't only need
to destroy damaged cells.

157
00:10:26,461 --> 00:10:29,011
It also needs to keep healthy cells alive.

158
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:31,490
Every day, cells are damaged.

159
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:33,890
Every day, cells are replaced.

160
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:35,990
Your tissues need to regenerate.

161
00:10:36,760 --> 00:10:38,610
Your immune system needs to function.

162
00:10:39,456 --> 00:10:40,946
Your wounds need to heal.

163
00:10:41,716 --> 00:10:43,746
Your organs need to maintain themselves.

164
00:10:44,896 --> 00:10:48,166
If we simply made p53
permanently hyperactive,

165
00:10:48,376 --> 00:10:50,706
we could destroy perfectly useful cells.

166
00:10:51,567 --> 00:10:54,057
So cancer protection is a balancing act.

167
00:10:54,687 --> 00:10:56,777
Too little control, and you get cancer.

168
00:10:57,745 --> 00:11:00,325
Too much control, and
you get damaged tissue,

169
00:11:00,405 --> 00:11:03,795
impaired regeneration, or
other biological problems.

170
00:11:04,305 --> 00:11:06,875
Evolution has to find the sweet spot.

171
00:11:09,585 --> 00:11:12,615
And humans are already trying
to manipulate this system.

172
00:11:13,485 --> 00:11:15,355
This isn't just theoretical biology.

173
00:11:16,509 --> 00:11:22,039
Scientists are actively studying the p53
pathway as a target for cancer treatment.

174
00:11:22,629 --> 00:11:26,509
One strategy is to restore
or reactivate p53 in

175
00:11:26,589 --> 00:11:29,319
cancer cells where the
pathway has been damaged.

176
00:11:30,325 --> 00:11:33,875
Another is to manipulate
proteins that regulate p53.

177
00:11:34,965 --> 00:11:38,755
One particularly important
regulator is MDM2.

178
00:11:39,489 --> 00:11:43,839
MDM2 normally helps
keep p53 under control.

179
00:11:44,669 --> 00:11:46,989
And researchers are
investigating how the human

180
00:11:47,069 --> 00:11:49,759
and elephant versions of
this regulator differ.

181
00:11:50,489 --> 00:11:52,869
Recent experiments have
even tested elephant

182
00:11:52,949 --> 00:11:56,399
MDM2 variants in human cellular models.

183
00:11:57,283 --> 00:11:59,783
So the elephant isn't
necessarily going to donate

184
00:11:59,863 --> 00:12:03,133
a single magical anti-cancer
gene to humanity.

185
00:12:04,014 --> 00:12:06,944
Instead, it may give scientists
something more valuable.

186
00:12:07,754 --> 00:12:10,224
New ideas about how to control the system.

187
00:12:12,684 --> 00:12:15,394
And here's where we can
return to Peto's paradox.

188
00:12:16,304 --> 00:12:20,574
Elephants aren't the only animals that
have evolved unusual cancer defences.

189
00:12:21,593 --> 00:12:25,523
Different long-lived and large-bodied
species have found different solutions.

190
00:12:26,593 --> 00:12:28,143
Some alter DNA repair.

191
00:12:29,013 --> 00:12:31,063
Some change cell-cycle control.

192
00:12:31,911 --> 00:12:36,021
Some appear to have unusual mechanisms
for eliminating damaged cells.

193
00:12:36,771 --> 00:12:40,401
Some have evolved changes in
metabolism or tissue organisation.

194
00:12:42,121 --> 00:12:43,741
Evolution has been running a gigantic

195
00:12:43,821 --> 00:12:46,771
experiment for hundreds
of millions of years.

196
00:12:47,301 --> 00:12:50,711
There isn't necessarily one
universal solution to cancer.

197
00:12:53,601 --> 00:12:56,491
And perhaps this is the
deepest part of the story.

198
00:12:57,241 --> 00:12:59,711
Cancer isn't really an external enemy.

199
00:13:00,381 --> 00:13:03,471
It's a consequence of something
fundamental about life.

200
00:13:04,304 --> 00:13:06,494
Your body is made of trillions of cells.

201
00:13:07,404 --> 00:13:08,994
Those cells have to divide.

202
00:13:09,764 --> 00:13:12,054
They have to copy enormous amounts of DNA.

203
00:13:13,044 --> 00:13:17,214
They have to survive. They have
to respond to their environment.

204
00:13:17,324 --> 00:13:20,264
And every time a cell
reproduces, evolution

205
00:13:20,344 --> 00:13:22,934
is happening on a microscopic scale.

206
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:27,210
Cancer is essentially evolution
happening inside your body.

207
00:13:30,080 --> 00:13:32,910
A cell acquires a mutation. Then another.

208
00:13:33,330 --> 00:13:36,030
A cell that grows faster
gets an advantage.

209
00:13:36,877 --> 00:13:39,587
A cell that ignores a
signal gets an advantage.

210
00:13:40,537 --> 00:13:43,067
A cell that avoids
death gets an advantage.

211
00:13:43,817 --> 00:13:46,367
And eventually, the result
can become a tumour.

212
00:13:47,003 --> 00:13:51,613
Our bodies therefore have to fight an
evolutionary process occurring inside us.

213
00:13:52,403 --> 00:13:56,393
And p53 is one of the mechanisms
we have evolved to fight it.

214
00:13:58,783 --> 00:14:01,973
So could we become more
like elephants? Maybe.

215
00:14:02,683 --> 00:14:05,023
But probably not by
simply inserting nineteen

216
00:14:05,103 --> 00:14:08,713
extra copies of TP53
into every human cell.

217
00:14:09,484 --> 00:14:11,914
The future is likely
to be much more subtle.

218
00:14:12,784 --> 00:14:17,174
We might learn how elephant
p53 interacts with MDM2.

219
00:14:17,914 --> 00:14:22,174
We might discover completely different
anti-cancer mechanisms in other animals.

220
00:14:23,003 --> 00:14:25,343
And eventually, we might
combine several of these

221
00:14:25,423 --> 00:14:28,673
ideas into new forms of cancer
prevention or treatment.

222
00:14:29,183 --> 00:14:30,913
But we're not there yet.

223
00:14:31,698 --> 00:14:34,378
And perhaps the most exciting
thing is that we don't

224
00:14:34,458 --> 00:14:37,308
even know all the tricks
nature has already invented.

225
00:14:38,258 --> 00:14:40,248
The elephant has changed the question.

226
00:14:41,198 --> 00:14:44,208
For a long time, the question
was, why do we get cancer?

227
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:47,710
But Peto's paradox gives
us another question.

228
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:51,610
Why don't elephants get as
much cancer as they should?

229
00:14:52,406 --> 00:14:54,856
And that question is incredibly powerful.

230
00:14:55,906 --> 00:14:58,126
Because nature has already
produced animals that are

231
00:14:58,206 --> 00:15:01,956
enormous, long-lived, and
remarkably resistant to cancer.

232
00:15:02,872 --> 00:15:07,362
We don't have to invent the idea
from scratch. We can study them.

233
00:15:07,932 --> 00:15:10,222
We can understand what evolution changed.

234
00:15:11,143 --> 00:15:14,423
And perhaps one day, we will be able
to borrow some of those solutions.

235
00:15:14,503 --> 00:15:17,233
Not to turn humans into elephants.

236
00:15:18,321 --> 00:15:22,171
But to make our own cells a little
better at one of life's oldest problems.

237
00:15:22,741 --> 00:15:24,611
Knowing when to repair themselves...

238
00:15:24,981 --> 00:15:27,351
and knowing when it's time to die.

239
00:15:28,071 --> 00:15:31,341
Nature may have already solved
parts of the cancer problem.

240
00:15:31,891 --> 00:15:34,781
We just haven't figured out
all of its solutions yet.
